23.3  PATH and command resolution

TL;DR
  - PATH is a list of folders separated by :. The shell looks for a command in
    each, left to right, and runs the first match.
  - type NAME says what a name is: alias, function, builtin or file. type -a
    shows every match.
  - Your own scripts usually go in ~/.local/bin. Many distros add it to PATH
    when it exists; otherwise add it in a startup file (27.2).

      echo "$PATH" | tr : '\n'
      type -a python3
      export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

On your machine
  PATH      /home/alice/.local/bin
            /usr/local/bin (doesn't exist)
            /usr/bin
            . ⚠ the current folder, wherever you are
  Repeated  /usr/bin (listed more than once: harmless, the first one wins)
  Libraries LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/oracle/lib
  Runs      programs built for x86-64, glibc

Pitfalls
  - bash remembers where it found each command: after installing or moving one,
    hash -r makes it search again.
  - An empty entry or . in PATH means "the current folder": a file named ls
    planted there would run instead of the real one.
  - which is a separate program that doesn't know your aliases and functions;
    type tells what your shell will actually run.
  - sudo uses its own PATH (secure_path in sudoers): a command your shell finds
    can be "not found" under sudo.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 23.3 --why

Related
  Commands  type, which
  Paths     /usr/local/bin, ~/.local/bin
  See also  atfq 23.2 ELF and "command not found"
  See also  atfq 27.2 Shell startup files
